  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The inventory level $I,$ in hundreds of units, of a medical supply $t$ weeks after ordering begins, is modeled by\n\n$$\nI(t)={t}^{2}{e}^{-t}+12,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level as a function of $t?$ \n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is\n{{field-1}} hundreds of units per week.",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is ${I}^{\u2032}(t).$\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}+12] \\\\  & =2t{e}^{-t}+{t}^{2}\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =2t{e}^{-t}-{t}^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}(2-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is $t{e}^{-t}(2-t)$ hundreds of units per week.",
